hypothesis_statement:
Let $p$ be a prime ([[def-prime]]), let $a \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $a 
e 0$, and write $v := v_p(a)$ ([[def-p-adic-valuation]]). Powers are the natural powers of [[def-group-power]] in the commutative monoid $(\mathbb{Z},\cdot,1)$ ([[lem-units-of-z]]), and $k + 1$ means $\sigma(k)$. Then:  0. $p^{k} \ge 1$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$; in particular $p^{k} > 0$ and    $p^{k} 
e 0$; 1. $p^{v} \mid a$ and $p^{v+1} 
mid a$; 2. for $k \in \mathbb{N}$: $p^{k} \mid a$ if and only if $k \le v$; 3. $a = p^{v} a'$ for some $a' \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $a' 
e 0$ and $p 
mid a'$; 4. $v \ge 1$ if and only if $p \mid a$; 5. $v_p(1) = v_p(-1) = 0$ and $v_p(p) = 1$.

Let $
u$ be a complex measure on $(X,\mathcal A)$. Then the set functions $$\operatorname{Re}
u(E):=\operatorname{Re}(
u(E)),\qquad \operatorname{Im}
u(E):=\operatorname{Im}(
u(E))$$ are finite signed measures on $(X,\mathcal A)$, and $$
u(E)=\operatorname{Re}
u(E)+i\,\operatorname{Im}
u(E)\qquad(E\in\mathcal A).$$

For all $n, k \in \mathbb{N}$:  1. **Pascal's rule.**    $\displaystyle inom{n+1}{k+1} =  inom{n}{k} +  inom{n}{k+1}$, with **no**    restriction relating $k$ to $n$; 2. **The hockey-stick identity.**    $\displaystyle\sum_{i<n+1} inom{i}{k} =  inom{n+1}{k+1}$, the sum being the    $\mathbb{N}$-valued finite sum of [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]] over    $i = 0, 1, \dots, n$.  ```tikz  egin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]   
ode at (0,3.8) {$|A|=n+1$};   
ode[draw,rounded corners] (all) at (0,3) {$[A]^{k+1}$, with $A'=A\setminus\{a\}$};   
ode[draw,rounded corners] (yes) at (-3,1.75) {$P=\{S:a\in S\}$};   
ode[draw,rounded corners] (no) at (3,1.75) {$Q=\{S:a
otin S\}$};   
ode[draw,rounded corners] (left) at (-3,0.35) {$[A']^k$};   
ode[draw,rounded corners] (right) at (3,0.35) {$[A']^{k+1}$};   \draw[->] (all) -- (yes);   \draw[->] (all) -- (no);   \draw[->] (yes) -- node[left] {$S\mapsto S\setminus\{a\}$} (left);   \draw[->] (no) -- node[right] {$S\mapsto S$} (right);   
ode at (0,-0.75) {${n+1\choose k+1}={n\choose k}+{n\choose k+1}$}; \end{tikzpicture} ```

1. If $A$ and $B$ are finite then $A 	imes B$ is finite and    $\lvert A 	imes Bvert = \lvert Avert \cdot \lvert Bvert$    ([[def-finite-cardinality]]). 2. Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $A_0, \dots, A_{m-1}$ be finite sets. Write    $$\prod_{i<m} A_i := \{\, f : f 	ext{ is a function with domain } m 	ext{ and } f(i) \in A_i 	ext{ for every } i < m \,\}.$$    Then $\prod_{i<m} A_i$ is finite and    $ ig\lvert\prod_{i<m} A_i igvert = \prod_{i<m}\lvert A_ivert$, the    right-hand product being the $\mathbb{N}$-valued one of    [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]].  At $m = 0$ clause 2 reads $ ig\lvert\prod_{i<0} A_i igvert = 1$: there is exactly one function with domain $\varnothing$, the empty function, and the empty product is $1$. Both sides are computed, not stipulated.  ```tikz  egin{tikzpicture}[x=1.45cm,y=0.9cm]   
ode at (0,2.45) {$a_0$};   
ode at (1,2.45) {$a_1$};   
ode at (2,2.45) {$a_2$};   
ode at (-1.1,1.5) {$b_0$};   
ode at (-1.1,0.5) {$b_1$};   \draw (-0.5,0) rectangle (2.5,2);   \draw (0.5,0) -- (0.5,2);   \draw (1.5,0) -- (1.5,2);   \draw (-0.5,1) -- (2.5,1);   
ode at (0,1.5) {$(a_0,b_0)$};   
ode at (1,1.5) {$(a_1,b_0)$};   
ode at (2,1.5) {$(a_2,b_0)$};   
ode at (0,0.5) {$(a_0,b_1)$};   
ode at (1,0.5) {$(a_1,b_1)$};   
ode at (2,0.5) {$(a_2,b_1)$};   
ode at (1,-0.6) {$|A	imes B|=3\cdot2=6$}; \end{tikzpicture} ```

1. **Two blocks.** If $A$ and $B$ are finite and **disjoint**, then $A \cup B$ is    finite and $\lvert A \cup Bvert = \lvert Avert + \lvert Bvert$    ([[def-finite-cardinality]]). 2. **A finite partition.** If $I$ is a finite set and $(A_i)_{i \in I}$ is a    family of finite sets that are pairwise disjoint, then $ igcup_{i \in I} A_i$    is finite and    $ ig\lvert igcup_{i \in I} A_i igvert = \sum_{i \in I}\lvert A_ivert$,    the sum being that of [[def-sum-over-a-finite-index-set]]. 3. **Splitting a sum along a partition of its index set.** Let $S$ be finite, let    $J$ be finite, and let $(S_j)_{j \in J}$ be pairwise disjoint subsets of $S$    with $ igcup_{j \in J} S_j = S$. Then for $a : S 	o \mathbb{R}$ or    $a : S 	o \mathbb{N}$,    $$\sum_{i \in S} a_i = \sum_{j \in J}\Big(\sum_{i \in S_j} a_i\Big), \qquad \prod_{i \in S} a_i = \prod_{j \in J}\Big(\prod_{i \in S_j} a_i\Big).$$    In particular $\sum_{i \in S \cup T} a_i = \sum_{i \in S} a_i + \sum_{i \in T} a_i$    for disjoint finite $S$ and $T$.  **Disjointness is a hypothesis and not a formality.** It is spent at exactly one step, the injectivity of the splice map, and dropping it makes clause 1 false; the companion page carries that false statement with its smallest witness.  ```tikz  egin{tikzpicture}[x=1.05cm,y=1cm]   
ode at (1,2.45) {$A$};   
ode at (5.2,2.45) {$B$};   
ode at (3.1,2.45) {$p,q>0$};   
ode (f0) at (0,1.55) {$f(0)$};   
ode (fd) at (1,1.55) {$\cdots$};   
ode (fp) at (2,1.55) {$f(p-1)$};   
ode (g0) at (4.2,1.55) {$g(0)$};   
ode (gd) at (5.2,1.55) {$\cdots$};   
ode (gq) at (6.2,1.55) {$g(q-1)$};   
ode (d0) at (0,0) {$0$};   
ode (dd1) at (1,0) {$\cdots$};   
ode (dp) at (2,0) {$p-1$};   
ode (dpp) at (4.2,0) {$p$};   
ode (dd2) at (5.2,0) {$\cdots$};   
ode (dpq) at (6.2,0) {$p+q-1$};   \draw[->] (d0) -- (f0);   \draw[->] (dp) -- (fp);   \draw[->] (dpp) -- (g0);   \draw[->] (dpq) -- (gq);   \draw[dashed] (3.1,-0.45) -- (3.1,2.15);   
ode at (1,-0.65) {$h(k)=f(k)$};   
ode at (5.2,-0.65) {$h(p+j)=g(j)$};   
ode at (3.1,-1.2) {$h:p+q\longrightarrow A\cup B$}; \end{tikzpicture} ```
